> Exact work and complete inverse guards

For each encoded byte with rank r, linear search performs r+1 equality tests and
movement performs r right shifts. Thus comparisons equal shifts plus n. Decode
indexes its supplied rank directly, so its search-comparison count is zero while
its movement count is identical. This does not mean decoding performs no work.
The256 initial stores, output allocations and direct loads are outside those
counters.

With alphabet size256 fixed, worst-case work is O(256n), linear in stream
length, and the driver alphabet occupies256 bytes. Outputs require n elements;
optional trace adds n fixed-size records and retains the original stream. The
complete capture quota counts input, output and step entries, exactly3n;
default1000000, zero disables. A nonzero insufficient cap throws before copying
or transformation. Every decoder rank is validated in0..255 before quota checks
or indexing, correcting the old unchecked invalid-index behavior.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mtf_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/move_to_front.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"empty", "repeat", "mixed", "binary"}, inputs{"", "AAAA", "ABBA", std::string("\0\xff\0\xff", 4)};
    std::vector<double> comparisons;
    std::print("| case | bytes | encoder comparisons | encoder shifts | decoder search comparisons | " "decoder shifts | round trip |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < inputs.size(); ++i) {
        auto e = dm::algorithms::mtf_encode_analyze(inputs[i]);
        auto d = dm::algorithms::mtf_decode_analyze(e.indices);
        comparisons.push_back(e.comparisons);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i],
                   inputs[i].size(), e.comparisons, e.shifts, d.comparisons,
                   d.shifts, d.bytes == inputs[i]);
    }
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(comparisons, labels,
                                     "actual equality tests");
    p.title("Move-to-front: actual encoder search work").x_label("byte fixture").y_label("alphabet equality tests").save_svg("build/mtf_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | bytes | encoder comparisons | encoder shifts | decoder search comparisons | decoder shifts | round trip |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| empty | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | true |
| repeat | 4 | 69 | 65 | 0 | 65 | true |
| mixed | 4 | 136 | 132 | 0 | 132 | true |
| binary | 4 | 261 | 257 | 0 | 257 | true |
\image(figures/mtf_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual encoder search comparisons, including binary and empty inputs.)
\alttext(The table also records inverse movement work and full-byte round trips; zero search comparisons is not zero decoder work.)

> Conclusion and references

The shared alphabet invariant proves lossless inversion. Actual move records
explain the small ranks that repetition produces, and the original BWT example
demonstrates an order-sensitive distribution change. The histogram and counters
describe recoding behavior without treating a vector of integer ranks as a
compressed representation. Invalid inverse ranks now fail before indexing, and
owned traces expose actual producer decisions.

[Bentley and colleagues, A locally adaptive data compression scheme (1986)|https://doi.org/10.1145/5684.5688]
describes move-to-front recency coding.
[Burrows and Wheeler, A Block-sorting Lossless Data Compression Algorithm (1994)|https://www.cs.jhu.edu/~langmea/resources/burrows_wheeler.pdf]
supplies the block-sorting context; this report uses the native cyclic
transform's explicit primary-row contract.

